<chapter id="stkclo">
#title("Stack-Allocated Closure-Functions")

<!-- ****** ****** -->

#para("\

Higher-order functions are a very convenient programming feature for
supporting certain forms of code reuse. Often a function passed as an
argument to a higher-order function call is a closure-function created on
heap at run-time, and it is most likely of no more use after the call.
Since every closure-function on heap is linear in this dialect of ATS2
(there is no garbage collector), it needs to be freed explicitly by the
programmer (or a type-error would occur during typechecking).

")#comment("para")

#para("\

Creating heap-allocated closure-functions implies the need for dynamic
memory allocation (DMA). In a restricted environment (e.g., one for
embedded programming), DMA may not be (fully) supported. Even when it is,
allocating a closure just to free it right after the call is wasteful. One
option for constructing a closure-function in the absence of DMA is to
store it in the stack-frame of the calling function, and there is special
syntax in ATS for doing so.

")#comment("para")

#para('\

Let us see a concrete example of stack-allocated closure.
The following code implements a higher-order function template that takes
such a closure:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
{res:t@ype}
ifold{n:nat}
(
  n: int(n)
, fopr: &(res, natLt(n)) -<clo1> res, ini: res
) : res = let
//
fun
loop
{i:nat | i <= n} .<n-i>.
(
  i: int(i)
, fopr: &(res, natLt(n)) -<clo1> res, res: res
) : res =
  if i < n
    then loop(i+1, fopr, fopr(res, i)) else res
  // end of [if]
//
in
  loop(0, fopr, ini)
end // end of [ifold]
//
')

Essentially, #dyncode("ifold(n, fopr, ini)") evaluates the expression
#dyncode("fopr(...fopr(fopr(ini, 0), 1)..., n-1)").  Note that the second
argument of #dyncode("ifold") is call-by-reference. The annotated arrow
#dyncode("-&lt;clo1&gt;") is used to form function types for
unboxed closures. So only a left value (representing some unboxed closure)
can be passed as the second argument to a call to #dyncode("ifold"), and
what is really passed at run-time is the address of the left value.

')#comment("para")

#para('\

For instance, the function #dyncode("dotprod") for computing the dot
product (or inner product) of two vectors can be implemented with a call to
#dyncode("ifold"). Here the vectors are flat arrays that are given by the
addresses of their first elements (the arrays can be located in a
stack-frame or in a block that is allocated by #dyncode("malloc")), and the
macro #dyncode("get_at") reads an element through such an address:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
typedef res = double
//
macdef
get_at(A, i) = $UNSAFE.ptr0_get_at_int<res>(,(A), ,(i))
//
')

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
dotprod
  {n:nat}
(
  n: int(n)
, A: ptr, B: ptr
) : res = let
//
var
fopr =
lam@(res: res, i: natLt(n)): res => res + get_at(A, i)*get_at(B, i)
//
in
  ifold<res>(n, fopr, 0.0)
end // end of [dotprod]
//
')

The keyword #keycode("lam@") (instead of #keycode("lam")) initiates the
creation of an unboxed closure at a given location. In the above case, the
variable #dyncode("fopr") is located in the stack-frame of
#dyncode("dotprod"), and the created closure is stored in the memory
reserved for #dyncode("fopr"). It is the obligation of the compiler of ATS
to make sure that the memory is large enough for storing the closure. No
memory is allocated on heap, and there is nothing to free: the closure
disappears when the call to #dyncode("dotprod") returns.

')#comment("para")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("chap_stkclo.dats", "on-line") the entirety of the
code used in this chapter plus some testing code.\

")#comment("para")

<!-- ****** ****** -->

</chapter><!--"stkclo"-->

